                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I have a lot of mixed thoughts on Deadly Sin. ON the 1 hand, it is rather impressive what was done with it back in 2009. On the other hand, even at the time I wasn't that crazy about it as it was doing what older console games were doing for years at that point. So even by today's standards, it isn't that impressive.  \n\nStop me if you heard this plot before: girl loses parents. Girl lives in the forest with her adoptive uncle. Girl gets letter from said uncle to brave the dangers to see an old friend. Girl learns her heritage isn't what it seems. Sounds super familiar, right? And that is how I would describe Deadly Sin in general: familiar.\n\nThe combat is generic as well. Character's take turns attacking until someone wins. Each turn, a character generates some sort of energy / MP to use spells and skills.  Characters gain money, experience, and skill points after battle. Skill points are used to learn new spells / skills. Learning skills is what ultimately allows characters to learn more powerful ones. And it isn't the character's levels that dictate what they can learn: It's the ultimately the number of skills / spells learned.\n\nIn general, it's a fine combat system. The problem  with it is that it is a slog. Enemies have high HP pools while taking little damage from the characters. There doesn't seem to be a clear formula to maximize damage. Characters either do a lot of damage or they don't; they receive a lot of damage or they don't. What should be a quick battle is a 5 minute slog. Most enemies -- even bosses (this is how to defeat the last 2 bosses with minimal difficulty)-- are weak to the stun status effect. So once a boss suffers it, it's just a matter of time until the boss is defeated. \n\nGraphics are a mixed bag as well. The tileset is clearly from the RPG maker engine. However, combat is seen from the party's back. So each of the characters are large and have some sort of animation to them. \n\nSonically, the game contains a lot of the same sounds from the engine. So if you've played other games made in it, you'll likely recognize them. The music, on the other hand, fits the setting rather well.\n\nIt took me around 25 hours to beat this game. And as much as I carp on it and talk about how generic it is, it was an indie darling back in 2009. It impressed a lot of reviewers. It's somewhat easy to see why as it has a lot of charm to it, and it shows off the talents of a new studio.  I picked this game up on sale for a dollar or 2. And that is the perfect price for it.",
